# Summary
Introduced an availability bug: persistent read-handle sessions retain unbounded per-connection state for attacker-controlled operation IDs and locations until release or disconnect.
StorageNodeServer now keeps a StorageNodeSession alive for the lifetime of a Unix-stream connection and stores every distinct acquired read_operation_id in a BTreeMap. The acquire path increments shared handle counts and inserts the request locations into the per-session read_operations map, but there is no maximum number of live read operations per session, no aggregate read-handle limit, and no timeout/lease expiry. Cleanup only happens on explicit ReadHandlesRelease or when the connection closes and Drop runs. A malicious or buggy local client with access to the private storage-node socket can send many ReadHandlesAcquire requests with unique IDs and keep the connection open, growing memory without bound and potentially exhausting the storage-node process. The previous implementation handled only one frame and returned UnsupportedOperation for ReadHandlesAcquire, so this persistent unbounded state is introduced by this commit.

# Resolution
Fixed in `aa2eb97 Bound storage node read handle resources`.

The fix adds finite resource controls at both the storage RPC protocol boundary and the storage-node session boundary:
- `ReadHandlesAcquire` and `ReadHandlesRelease` now have per-message payload caps enforced by the frame reader before allocating the payload buffer.
- Read operation IDs are capped at 256 bytes and decoded through a bounded string reader, so oversized IDs are rejected before copying into a `String`.
- A single acquire request is capped at 1024 shard locations.
- Each storage-node session is capped at 4096 live read operations.
- Each storage-node process is capped at 16k aggregate live read operations and 64k aggregate retained read-handle locations.
- The storage-node listener now tracks active sessions and caps long-lived accepted sessions at 1024, preventing unbounded thread/session growth.

The fix returns `StorageRpcErrorCode::ResourceExhausted` for storage-node resource quota exhaustion while preserving idempotent acquire/release behavior for valid retry traffic. Regression coverage was added for oversized frame rejection before allocation, oversized operation ID rejection before copy, per-session read-operation exhaustion, aggregate read-handle exhaustion, and active-session limit accounting.

