# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the commit introduced persistent per-connection sessions rather than one-frame handling: `handle_session` loops with one `StorageNodeSession` until EOF/error.
- [x] Confirm attacker-controlled `ReadHandlesAcquire` messages reach session state over the real Unix-stream RPC path.
- [x] Confirm distinct `read_operation_id` values are inserted into an unbounded per-session `BTreeMap` and increment retained shared handle counts.
- [x] Confirm cleanup is limited to explicit `ReadHandlesRelease` or disconnect/drop, with no timeout, lease expiry, or quota.
- [x] Dynamically demonstrate accumulation and availability impact with a PoC, plus crash/debugger evidence.
## Report
Validated the finding. Code review confirmed that `StorageNodeServer::handle_session` creates one `StorageNodeSession` and loops on the same Unix-stream connection until EOF/error (`crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:200-219`). `ReadHandlesAcquire` is dispatched into that session (`storage_node_server.rs:244-282`). Each new `read_operation_id` calls shared handle `acquire` and inserts into `read_operations: BTreeMap<String, SessionReadHandle>` with cloned locations, with no cap (`storage_node_server.rs:456-500`). Cleanup only happens on explicit release or `Drop` at disconnect (`storage_node_server.rs:503-529`). The protocol validator only rejects empty IDs; it does not limit ID length, per-session count, aggregate count, or lifetime (`crates/storage/src/storage_rpc.rs:927-943`).

I added a targeted unit-test PoC that drives the real `StorageNodeServer` over its Unix socket and sends many distinct `ReadHandlesAcquire` requests without release. Bounded run output: `retained_read_handles_while_connection_open=1024` followed by `retained_read_handles_after_disconnect=0`, proving per-session state accumulates while the attacker-controlled connection stays open and is released only on disconnect.

Direct availability crash attempt succeeded under constrained memory: running the same PoC with `POC_OPS=30000 POC_ID_BYTES=20000` and `ulimit -v 350000` aborted with `memory allocation of 20043 bytes failed` and exit 134. This demonstrates the unbounded retained state can exhaust process memory. Valgrind/GDB were attempted but unavailable in the container (`bash: command not found: valgrind`, `bash: command not found: gdb`). I also captured an LLDB trace showing the actual server thread path: `handle_session` -> `dispatch_frame` -> `read_handles_acquire_response` -> `StorageNodeSession::acquire_read_handles` stopped at `self.read_operations.insert(...)` on `storage_node_server.rs:493`.

