# Attack-path analysis
Final: ignore | Decider: model_decided | Matrix severity: ignore | Policy adjusted: ignore
## Rationale
Adjusted from medium to ignore for the primary project scope. The resource-exhaustion bug itself is real and validated, but static service mapping shows the main deployed artifact, argmin-s3, opens local storage nodes directly and serves HTTP; it does not start StorageNodeServer or expose the vulnerable Unix-socket RPC path. Even in a custom deployment, the attack is local/internal and gated by a private socket directory, and the impact is only single-process availability. This does not meet the threat model's important remote S3 attack surface and does not justify medium security criticality for the main product.
## Likelihood
low - The vulnerable code is not invoked by the main argmin-s3 binary, no production socket exposure artifact was found, and exploitation requires local access to a private Unix-socket directory. It is straightforward only in a custom deployment that explicitly starts this server and grants a client socket access.
## Impact
low - If StorageNodeServer is manually deployed and a local client can access its private Unix socket, the client can consume memory and potentially crash or wedge that one storage-node process. The demonstrated impact is availability-only; there is no data disclosure, integrity impact, privilege escalation, or remote HTTP reachability in the main product.
## Assumptions
- The primary deployed product in this repository is the argmin-s3 binary described by the threat model.
- A deployment would have to explicitly instantiate StorageNodeServer::bind for the vulnerable Unix-socket service to exist; the checked main binary does not do this.
- If StorageNodeServer is deployed, Unix-socket directory permissions are intended to restrict access to local trusted users or the service account.
- No cloud, Kubernetes, systemd, or other deployment artifact in the checked repository exposes the StorageNodeServer Unix socket to remote users.
- StorageNodeServer::bind must be used by a running deployment, which is not done by crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs
- The attacker must have local filesystem access to the private storage-node Unix socket directory, effectively the socket owner or equivalent local privilege
- The attacker must keep a Unix-stream connection open and send many ReadHandlesAcquire frames with distinct non-empty read_operation_id values
## Path
local client -> private Unix socket -> handle_session loop -> ReadHandlesAcquire -> unbounded BTreeMap entries -> storage-node memory exhaustion
## Path evidence
- `crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:200-219` - One StorageNodeSession is created and reused inside a loop for the lifetime of the UnixStream, so per-session state persists while the client keeps the connection open.
- `crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:244-282` - ReadHandlesAcquire frames are decoded and dispatched into the persistent session.
- `crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:457-500` - Each new read_operation_id is stored in read_operations: BTreeMap<String, SessionReadHandle> with cloned locations and no visible quota.
- `crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:503-529` - State is removed only by explicit release or by Drop when the session ends.
- `crates/storage/src/storage_rpc.rs:927-943` - Protocol validation only rejects empty read operation IDs; it does not cap ID length, live operation count, or lifetime.
- `crates/storage/src/storage_node_server.rs:640-665` - The storage-node socket path must be in a private directory, reducing reachability to local/internal users with filesystem access.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:153-158` - The main product binary opens local storage nodes directly through StorageCluster::open_local_nodes rather than binding StorageNodeServer.
- `crates/argmin-s3/src/main.rs:222-240` - The main runtime serves the HTTP frontend; this is the exposed product surface in the threat model, not the storage-node Unix socket.
- `crates/storage/src/lib.rs:38-40` - StorageNodeServer is exported as a storage crate module, but the RPC module is crate-private and no production entrypoint was found starting it.
## Narrative
The core bug is valid: a StorageNodeSession persists for the lifetime of a Unix-stream connection, ReadHandlesAcquire reaches that session, distinct attacker-controlled operation IDs are inserted into an unbounded BTreeMap, and cleanup occurs only on release or connection teardown. Earlier validation also demonstrated retained handles and memory-exhaustion under constrained memory. However, static repository mapping shows this server is not started by the main argmin-s3 binary, which instead opens local storage nodes directly and exposes only the S3 HTTP service. The Unix-socket server also requires a private socket directory. Therefore this is best treated as an out-of-scope or future/internal local DoS for the primary project threat model, not a medium externally reachable S3-service vulnerability.
## Controls
- Storage-node listener uses a Unix domain socket rather than a TCP port.
- Storage-node socket parent directory must be private: mode & 0o077 == 0.
- The primary argmin-s3 HTTP path uses separate controls such as SigV4 authentication and max connection/in-flight limits, but those controls do not apply to this unused Unix-socket server.
- No per-session read-handle quota, aggregate memory limit, read_operation_id length cap, or read-handle lease timeout is present in the affected code.
## Blindspots
- Static review cannot rule out downstream users or external deployment wrappers instantiating StorageNodeServer::bind outside this repository.
- No systemd, container, Kubernetes, or packaging manifests were found in scope to confirm how a future storage-node process would be deployed.
- Unix socket file mode after bind is not explicitly set in the reviewed lines; parent directory privacy is enforced, but operator ownership and umask still affect local access.
